TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "lineup coverage",
    "structural roles",
    "organizer",
    "shaper",
    "terminal",
    "talent",
    "player counts",
    "within-team variation"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "how to define/measure structural roles",
    "how to quantify lineup coverage",
    "how to demonstrate separability from talent",
    "how to control for talent",
    "how to measure within-team variation",
    "what constitutes 'talent control behaves'"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available from this project."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available, therefore it establishes nothing and leaves all aspects of the research question open."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The glossary defines structural role categories (Primary Organizer, Shaper, Terminal Scorer, Role Occupant/Specialist) based on usage and residual influence. Residual influence, in turn, is derived from structural influence (L), which measures how much a team's structural fingerprint changes when one player's initiations are removed. This suggests a pathway to assign roles to players, provided the methodology for calculating structural influence (L) and residual influence can be implemented from the available play-type initiation data.",
    "The data available (play-by-play, substitutions, derived play-type records) appears sufficient to construct lineups, identify initiators, calculate usage, and potentially derive structural influence, which are prerequisites for assigning structural roles and then assessing lineup coverage.",
    "The term 'talent control behaves' is interpreted as requiring that talent metrics, when included as controls, demonstrate expected relationships with outcomes and that the effects of lineup coverage remain discernible after accounting for talent."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise methodology for calculating 'structural influence (load, L)' and 'residual influence' for all players in all contexts (not just pre-absence spells) is not explicitly detailed in the glossary or provided data description. This is a critical missing piece for assigning roles consistently.",
    "The exact definition of 'talent' and 'counts' for control purposes is not specified, requiring a decision on which metrics to use (e.g., box score stats, advanced metrics, player value estimates)."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was available, so it did not change the decision."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.7,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "The derivation of 'structural influence (L)' and subsequent 'residual influence' for all players in all lineups is a complex, non-trivial step. If the methodology from the 'Structural Influence paper' relies heavily on actual player absence spells to calculate L, it may not be directly applicable to assign roles to every player in every lineup. A clear, reproducible method for calculating L in a general context is needed to ensure role assignment reliability."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work addressing this specific question was found in the project's historical records."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The operationalization of 'organizer, shaper, terminal' roles hinges on the calculation of 'structural influence' and 'residual influence'. If these measures are not robustly defined and consistently calculable across all players and time periods, the role assignments and subsequent lineup coverage metrics will be unreliable. The glossary's mention of 'L_pre... measured from games before an absence' suggests a context-specific calculation that might not generalize easily to all lineups."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "The question explicitly requires separability from 'talent/counts'. Accurately defining and controlling for player talent and basic player counts is crucial. Without robust talent controls, any observed effects of structural lineup coverage could be confounded by underlying differences in player quality or simply the number of high-skill players on the court."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "While the total possessions and team-seasons are substantial, the number of unique lineups satisfying the specific '>=1 organizer, shaper, terminal' coverage criterion, especially within individual teams for 'within-team variation' analysis, might be sparse. This could lead to insufficient statistical power for certain lineup combinations or for detecting subtle effects."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Some specific lineup configurations or coverage types might occur very rarely, making it difficult to draw statistically significant conclusions about their impact or variation."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "If the definition of 'talent' or 'counts' for control variables implicitly or explicitly incorporates aspects that are themselves influenced by structural roles, it could lead to circularity or an over-correction, making it difficult to demonstrate 'separability'."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.4,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.2,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The research aims to establish: 1) whether the presence of at least one Organizer, Shaper, and Terminal player varies within a team's lineups; 2) whether this lineup coverage provides explanatory power beyond player talent and simple player counts for lineup performance; and 3) whether the chosen talent control variables behave as expected (i.e., show significant association with performance and allow for the independent assessment of lineup coverage).",
    "unit": "The primary unit of analysis will be the lineup (defined by the 5 players on the court) aggregated at the game-level or stint-level. Player-level roles will be assigned based on their aggregated performance over a rolling window or season, then applied to lineups. The within-team variation will be assessed by comparing different lineup configurations or the same configurations across different game contexts for the same team.",
    "sample": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 complete regular seasons and playoffs, and NBA 2025-26 regular season (complete up to 2026-04-13) and playoffs (complete up to 2026-06-21). Data will include 420,993 possessions and 90 team-seasons. Garbage time possessions will be excluded. Lineups with minimum possession thresholds (e.g., 50 possessions per team-season) will be used to ensure sufficient sample size for lineup-level analysis.",
    "power": "Power analysis will be conducted to determine the minimum detectable effect size for the variations and separability claims, given the expected variance and sample sizes of lineups. This will inform the minimum possession thresholds for lineup inclusion. Given the number of possessions and team-seasons, statistical power is anticipated to be adequate for well-represented lineups, but may be limited for rare configurations.",
    "controls": "To test separability from 'talent/counts', player-level talent will be controlled using a combination of per-minute box-score statistics (e.g., points, assists, rebounds, true shooting percentage) and potentially advanced metrics (e.g., usage rate, assist percentage, turnover percentage) for each player in the lineup. Player counts will be controlled by including the number of players above certain percentile thresholds for key talent metrics (e.g., number of top-25% scorers, number of top-25% playmakers) in the lineup. Team-level controls (e.g., team quality, coach effects) will also be considered using fixed effects or dummy variables for team-season. The 'behavior' of talent controls will be assessed by their statistical significance and direction in regression models.",
    "falsification": "The research question can be falsified if: 1) no statistically significant within-team variation in lineup coverage (>=1 organizer, shaper, terminal) is observed; 2) lineup coverage metrics do not show incremental explanatory power for lineup performance (e.g., Offensive Rating, Net Rating) beyond robust talent and player count controls; or 3) the talent control variables do not exhibit expected relationships with performance, suggesting inadequate control for talent.",
    "multiplicity": "Multiple tests will be performed (e.g., testing within-team variation, testing separability, testing different performance outcomes). A clear alpha level (e.g., 0.05) will be set for each hypothesis, and adjustments for multiple comparisons (e.g., Bonferroni correction, False Discovery Rate control) will be considered if appropriate, particularly for exploratory analyses or if numerous role definitions are tested.",
    "decision_rule": "The research question will be considered 'supported' if all three components are statistically significant at p < 0.05 after appropriate controls and multiplicity adjustments: a) statistically significant within-team variation in lineup coverage, b) statistically significant incremental explanatory power of lineup coverage beyond talent/counts, and c) talent controls demonstrating expected relationships and statistical significance. It will be 'rejected' if these conditions are not met. If the core measure of structural roles cannot be reliably established from the data, the question will be deemed 'untestable'.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 (partial) as described in the task statement, using the latest available derived play-type possession records and NBA stats API data."
  }
}

